[{:seon.task/name "write-back-answers-what-happened"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #21 (notes/audit-2026-10-21/FENCES.md). seon.fx/write-file (fx.clj:36-44) spits to path.fx-PID then calls (.renameTo tmp path) and ignores its boolean: a failed rename (a missing directory, a cross-device path, a permission) answers {:value {:bytes n}} as success, leaves the tmp file, and the asker records a write-back that never reached disk. This is the loop's last step (merged code written back). Correct: rename(2) through jolt.ffi with errno; a failure answers :seon.fx/io-error {:path :tmp :errno :message} and removes the tmp; success answers the bytes and the path's size after the rename. Replaces the ignored .renameTo. Estimate: one syscall more than today; no cost proof (constant)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-failed-rename-is-an-io-error-and-leaves-no-tmp
                      seon.fx-test/a-write-back-answers-the-bytes-on-disk}}
 {:seon.task/name "merge-trains-off-as-decided"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #19. DECISIONS (Root's calls on R3's questions): merge trains stay off (:seon.config.merge/max-running 1) until one combined test run of all train members, the par-200 hang and root memory growth with concurrent merges are fixed. The code says 8: seon.reconcile/default-max-merging (reconcile.clj:21-25) went from 1 to 8 in b7459c3, whose message says trains are off by default, and nothing writes :seon.config.merge/max-running (seon.task/boot-tx, task.clj:88-92, writes only the agent dial), so every root runs trains of up to 8. Correct: the default is 1; boot-tx writes both dials as facts on main so the dial in force is a recorded fact a person can read (my.root/problems or the dashboard), and a train is turned on by writing the fact. Deletes the code default as the source of truth (the schema default in resources states it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/boot-records-merge-max-running-one
                      seon.reconcile-test/two-ready-disjoint-merges-run-one-at-a-time-by-default}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-spawn-is-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #20. When root refuses a spawn (`seon.root` answers its `:spawn` op with `:seon.root/refused`: the branch is held by a live agent, e.g. an outside attach, or `:seon.config.root/max-agents` agents are live), the reconciler's `:spawned` step in `seon.reconcile/advance` gets an answer with no `:id` and only ends the flight: no attempt, no fact, no problem. The task stays ready and is retried only when another commit wakes the reconciler, and `seon.reconcile/stuck` lists only stuck facts and unreleased tasks, so at quiescence a task that cannot start is invisible. Correct: the refusal is written on main as the attempt, status `:refused` with root's message as `:seon.attempt/reason` and the error map (as a dead attempt is), which needs `:refused` in `:seon.attempt/status` (change a schema at your REPL with `my.schema/register!`: the merge writes it back to its EDN file); `seon.reconcile/stuck` reports a task whose latest attempt is refused as a root cause (`{:task :cause the refusal message :attempt :blocks}`); the next pass starts it again as the next attempt. Replaces the silent `(done st)` in the `:spawned` step. Proofs written red by root 2026-10-21: the first fails because nothing is written, the second because the status schema refuses `:refused`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-spawn-writes-a-refused-attempt
                      seon.reconcile-test/quiescence-names-a-task-that-cannot-start}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-transient-model-failure-parks"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #28-29. (1) A provider 429 or a transport failure is answered to the agent (seon.fx/settle, fx.clj:68-82, then reply!) and seon.turn/work! ends the attempt :error on any error map; the judge then attaches a refinement chain, so the task's budget (default 2) is spent on the network. (2) The call itself is bounded by a clock: seon.model/openai-compat :or {timeout-ms 120000} and connect 10000 (model.clj:51,109); a DeepSeek reasoning reply past 120 s is a provider error, ending the attempt. Correct: the :model owner keeps a :seon.model/rate-limited request queued until its bucket refills (a dependency on the bucket, not a blind retry) and the asker parks; each 429 is a recorded fact; only a non-transient provider error answers the agent. The call streams (stream true, SSE): every chunk is progress, the call ends when the server ends it or the socket dies (the named dragon seon.model/http-post), and a stream with no chunk for long is an observed fact with bytes received, never a deadline; connect keeps a declared dial. Estimate: same tokens; one more parse per chunk."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-rate-limited-model-call-waits-for-the-bucket-and-is-answered
                      seon.turn-test/a-transient-provider-failure-does-not-end-the-attempt
                      seon.model-test/a-streamed-reply-ends-when-the-server-ends-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-test-bound-derived-and-named"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #22. The proof run's bound is the literal 30000 in seven places: seon.turn observe (turn.clj:43), the judge (reconcile.clj:649, 735), seon.agent handle :tests (agent.clj:385), seon.root/run-tests! and merge! (root.clj:688, 721), seon.web (web.clj:99, 101). Past it the child is killed and the run is one :error report with no test var, so every proof reads :none, the attempt is refused, and a refinement chain is spent; a task whose cost proofs measure large sizes can never go green, and the agent is told only that nothing ran. The agent polls the pipe in 1 s slices (agent.clj:332-337). Correct: one bound, declared once (a seon.config dial, :seon.config.agent/test-bound-ms on the task overriding it), or derived from the proofs' own cost estimates (sum over the reaching cost proofs of expected ns at their sizes times slack, plus a measured per-namespace load); the test child writes each test var to the pipe as it starts, so a kill answers a typed :seon.test/timed-out {:test :bound-ms :derived-from} that the context shows; the agent reads the pipe to EOF or the bound, not in slices. Deletes the seven literals."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-killed-test-run-names-the-test-that-was-running
                      seon.agent-test/the-test-bound-is-one-declared-value
                      seon.context-test/a-timed-out-run-shows-its-test-and-bound}}
 {:seon.task/name "budgets-are-data-and-spend-is-measured"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #23-25, #27, #30-31. (1) Dials are code literals: seon.reconcile default-max-turns 8, default-max-running 4, default-budget 2, merge-dials {:max-conflicts 3 :max-merge-turns 2} (reconcile.clj:13-33), seon.model/default-max-tokens 2048 (model.clj:59-62). The turn cap is how real attempts end: t5/out/real3.log, 4 of 9 work tasks ended :cap at 8 turns having spent 14k-70k tokens each; the paid resource is tokens, the cap counts turns. (2) Unknown spend is written as 0: judge-tx (reconcile.clj:603-605) records (:turns end 0), (:tokens end 0), :ms 0.0 when the agent wrote no end; parse-reply (model.clj:100) reads absent usage as 0 tokens; seon.fx settle (fx.clj:77-79) spends (or cost 1). Correct: the defaults are schema defaults in resources (seon.facts.edn) written by boot as config facts on main; an attempt's budget is stated in tokens (turns remain a dial, not the budget); the spend is root's :model owner's per-attempt count (it already keys :spent), not the agent's claim; an absent end is outcome :no-end with turns and tokens absent; absent usage is absent, an estimate is marked :estimated. The :cap reason names what was spent against what was allowed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fences/merge-trains-off-as-decided"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-attempt-with-no-end-records-no-fake-spend
                      seon.reconcile-test/an-attempt-ends-cap-when-its-token-budget-is-spent
                      seon.model-test/a-reply-without-usage-records-no-tokens}}
 {:seon.task/name "records-keep-the-exact-value"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #32-37. Values are cut by subs where they are stored, not where they are rendered (AGENTS: storage keeps the exact value; renders budget it): seon.agent/error-map cuts every exception message at message-chars 2000 (agent.clj:225-239); seon.model cuts an HTTP error body at 300 chars (model.clj:121), losing the provider's reason for a 400; seon.proofs/failures keeps the first max-assertions 8 failing assertions per test and drops the rest with no count (proofs.clj:45-58); seon.learn cuts a waste form at 80 chars (learn.clj:72); seon.agent-proc cuts the form at 200 chars into :last-op and :running (agent_proc.clj:72,75), stored in :seon.agent/stalled and :seon.agent/died on main; seon.root records a dead agent's stderr as its last tail-bytes 2048 (root.clj:64-76) though the file is kept beside the store. Correct: each record holds the whole value or a reference to it (the eval record's id and exact text; the stderr file's path and size; all assertions, or 8 plus :omitted n and the run's handle); the context and the dashboard render them under their budgets through the one printer. Deletes message-chars, max-assertions, tail-bytes and the four subs."
  :seon.task/kind :work
