 {:seon.task/name "the-compute-executor-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #2, the lead for seon.root/root-threads-are-a-known-number. Jolt's Flow runs :compute steps on clojure.core.async.impl.dispatch's compute-executor (reference-code/jolt/stdlib/clojure/core/async/impl/dispatch.clj:60): an unbounded newCachedThreadPool of unnamed daemon threads with a 60 s keep-alive, and bounds each step with .get compute-timeout-ms 5000 (flow/impl.clj:419); a step past 5 s throws into root-errors while its worker keeps running, and the next step takes another worker. seon.render.proc/render-proc is :compute. Fits the evidence: lifecycle 25,25,25,26,26,29x5 (target/check/20261021T110316), scale 100 root threads 38 -> 55 (20261021T105306), PROCESSES-draft's unattributed Thread-N. First prove or refute the hypothesis (name the threads: jstack-like dump through lib, or count compute workers before/after each cycle). If it holds: root passes create-flow a declared :compute-exec (a fixed pool of named threads, size a seon.config dial) and render's step bound becomes an observed overrun (its duration recorded) rather than an abandoned future. Estimate: root's thread count after a cycle = declared sum, exactly."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares
                      seon.render-test/a-slow-render-step-is-recorded-not-abandoned}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-is-answered-and-failover-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #11, #13. (1) seon.root/agent-ping (root.clj:801-803) maps any answer without :status to :unknown, including the :seon.root/no-agent error map, so a missing agent reads as unknown; agent-health and health (root.clj:790-796, 825-833) answer :unknown past 1000 ms. (2) The fork server's :standby-failed event (fork_server.clj:76, forwarded at :227) reaches seon.root/back-op, whose :ev case has no branch for it: dropped. The server then has no standby, and its next death is :server-lost with no recorded cause. Correct: a ping's answer passes through as itself (an error map stays an error map); :unknown only for a proc observed dead; a failed standby is a recorded platform problem (:seon.fork-server/standby-failed with :active-threads) and the view shows the server has no standby."
  :seon.task/kind :work
