                      seon.procs-test/a-failed-standby-is-a-recorded-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "leases-and-store-size-are-declared-bounds"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #17, #51. (1) Each agent mints entity ids from an AtomicLong set to its lease (seon.db/after-fork!, db.clj:117-122; lease = base + n*EID-LEASE, root.clj:173) and nothing stops it at lease + EID-LEASE (100,000): an agent minting more silently takes the next agent's ids, and two branches merge different entities under one id. (2) Real bounds are read outside seon.config: SEON_LMDB_MB 1024 (db.clj:60), SEON_TRIP_MB 4 and SEON_AGENT_HEAP_MB 1024 (agent.clj:25,29); with runs kept and one store of many branches the LMDB map fills, the writer refuses (named dragon) and nothing showed it coming. Correct: minting past the lease answers a typed :seon.db/lease-spent (an :err item in the agent's eval) or takes a fresh block through the writer, and the agent's stats show ids used of its lease; the three dials live in seon.config with reasons; (c/state) and the dashboard show the map's used and allotted bytes; the writer's refusal names the map size. Estimate: one comparison per minted id. Lane STORES' plan (2026-10-21, a Datahike change, waits for root's approval): the check belongs where the id is minted, beside the one that exists. reference-code/datahike src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc:58 next-eid already raises :entity-id/range when an id would reach tx0; the store's allocator (datahike.writing/eid-allocators, writing.cljc:841) gains a per-store ceiling for this process (an AtomicLong pair, or a ceilings map beside it), set by seon.db/after-fork! to lease + EID-LEASE, and next-eid raises {:error :entity-id/lease-spent :entity-id eid :ceiling c} past it, so the transaction is refused before anything applies (dw/apply! runs ahead, so a check after it would be too late). seon.agent/transact! reads the raise as :seon.db/lease-spent (an :err item naming the lease, its use and the fix). Root's own process has no ceiling (its writes start at lease-base below every agent's block). The dials: SEON_LMDB_MB, SEON_TRIP_MB, SEON_AGENT_HEAP_MB move into seon.config with their reasons; map use is the data.mdb length against the map size (LMDB grows the file as pages are used), in (c/state) :store and the writer's refusal."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/minting-past-the-lease-is-refused-not-shared
                      seon.db-test/the-store-reports-its-map-use}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-ends-on-a-defect-too"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #26. seon.e2e/await-quiet (e2e.clj:16-31; also the T5 harness, t5/src/t5/run.clj:154) waits on main's quiescence fact with no deadline, as the owner asked. But a flight whose answer is lost (a reconciler or api step throws: Flow keeps the old state and drops its outputs) never ends, quiescence never comes, and the run and bin/check hang forever with nothing recorded. Correct: the await also completes on the observed facts that end hope: a :platform problem recorded on main (seon.error, a root proc's defect) or a root proc observed dead, answering {:ended :defect :problem ...} which the run prints and fails on. No clock. Once seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took lands this path should not fire; it stays as the observation."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/await-quiet-ends-on-a-recorded-defect}}
 {:seon.task/name "checks-await-facts-and-assert-exact-numbers"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #1, #52-57. seon.check has 71 Thread/sleep and await-status sites: await-status polls c/state every 2 ms for 5-30 s and on timeout returns false while the check goes on; until (check.clj:190-192) polls my.root/problems every 100 ms for 120 s; sleeps make windows (smoke 200, robust 200/300, lifecycle 50, backpressure 5/400/500, scale 500/1000, hotreload 5x200; e2e fresh 300). bin/check orphan polls kill -0 once a second for 5 s (bin/check:72-80); the stall is provoked by (Thread/sleep 2500) against SEON_STALL_MS=1000. Tolerances are guesses: lifecycle threads <= 2 over cycle 2 (check.clj:848; 25 -> 29 went red), backpressure < 2000 arrived while paused (check.clj:1005; measured 109-629), robust < 25000 chars (check.clj:357). Correct: readiness and ends are facts (tap c/events for :ready/:dead, seon.e2e/await-db on main), a paused proc's state is its ping; orphans' deaths are kqueue EVFILT_PROC events; the stall is a form parked on a never-answered channel; each number is exact: threads equal root's declared count (seon.root/root-threads-are-a-known-number), backpressure's ceiling is AGENT-BUF + the end's :in + the frames the 1 MB kernel buffer holds + one per pump (frame bytes from seon.sock/frame), robust asserts the printer's :tokens <= the reply budget. Each check prints its derived number beside the measured one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.check/branches-as-in-production" "seon.root/root-threads-are-a-known-number"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/no-check-sleeps-or-polls
                      seon.check-test/backpressure-asserts-its-derived-ceiling}}
 {:seon.task/name "cost-tolerances-are-derived"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #38, #40-42, #44. seon.cost/slack {:ns 3.0 :bytes 1.25 :calls 1.0} (cost.clj:40-43): bytes are called exact yet get 25% because the unit table flakes (seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache: 11.6 vs 8.9 KB); ns gets 3x for load. steeper-by 0.3 (cost.clj:45-49) and ratchet 1.5 (cost.clj:55-59) are guesses too. seon.context's prompt hardcodes 1.25x/1.0x/3x (context.clj:27), a second copy. test/seon/profile_test.clj:37-41 asserts within 2x and is gated (when contract/on?), zero assertions when off. Correct: each unit row records its spread over k runs under a declared cache state; a dimension's slack is the spread of the units its estimate prices (bytes then near 1.0); ns carries the run's load (uptime, the bench lock) and its spread at that load; the exponent tolerance is derived per dimension and proof as log(slack_d)/log(n_max/n_min) (over 100x: bytes 1.25 -> 0.048, ns 3 -> 0.24); the ratchet is the overrun's own measured spread at filing; every verdict prints the derived tolerances, and the prompt renders them from seon.cost. profile_test asserts the sampling rule exactly (ceil(n/64) timed calls) in an environment with contracts on."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/the-exponent-tolerance-follows-slack-and-span
                      seon.cost-test/slack-is-the-units-measured-spread
                      seon.context-test/the-prompt-states-the-judges-own-tolerances}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-dragon-gate-sees-fences"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #59. bin/dragons greps (catch with awk, leaves check.clj and e2e.clj out, and cannot see the other ways a failure becomes a value: deref with a timeout and a fallback, alts! against a timeout, Thread/sleep, ping-proc :timeout-ms, .join with a bound. It printed DRAGONS OK (18 catch sites) while about 40 such sites stood (FENCES.md). Correct: the gate reads the compiler's resolved references per definition (the mechanism seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler records; the same one the intern/alter-var-root ban uses), lists every call of clojure.core/deref with 3 args, java.lang.Thread/sleep, clojure.core.async/timeout, clojure.core.async.flow/ping-proc, Thread .join and catch, in src and in the check drivers, and is red unless docs/DRAGONS.md names that function's row (a real dragon with its declared answer). Replaces the awk; the table gains a Fences section."
  :seon.task/kind :work
